:: Start [start] {"title":"Prologue / The Dead Bird Alarm","scene":"yard"}
# No Clean Getaway

@Chapter("2:17 A.M. · MARLOWE BAY", "The dead bird alarm")

The tow truck reverses into Marigold Court with a warning beep that sounds like a
dying electronic bird. It cries seven times, coughs once, and gives up.

You are **Lenora Vale**: organizer, landlady, survivor of three certificate programs
and one extremely public bankruptcy. From behind the curtain you watch **Mars Quinn**
climb down from the cab carrying a grocery bag. The bottom shows one bottle of vodka
and two tins of cat food.

Neither of you owns a cat.

::: dialogue {.dialogue speaker="Mars" mood="dry"}
“I saw the curtain move, Lenny.”
:::

::: dialogue {.dialogue speaker="Lenora" mood="sharp"}
“Only people I like call me Lenny.”
:::

Mars says she needs one night. She says her boyfriend Cal used the rent for a
“business emergency.” She says the tow truck is hers, except for the registration,
the insurance, the notice taped to the door, and one man named Arthur whom nobody
has seen since spring.

This is the first decision. Later, both women will insist it was the other's.

[[Open the door and call it solidarity -> OpenDoor]]

[[Make her admit she has nowhere else to go -> MakeHerAsk]]

[[Offer cash for a motel instead -> MotelMoney]]

:: OpenDoor {"title":"One Night / Structural Resources","scene":"kitchen"}
@do $trust += 2
@do $notes.push({id: $notes.length, text: "Lenora opened the door before Mars had to ask twice."})

@Chapter("ONE NIGHT", "Structural resources")

“A woman does not close a door on another woman in need,” you say, opening it.

Mars steps past you. “You were absolutely about to close it.”

“Thought is conditioned. Action reveals essence.”

“Where does my essence sleep?”

[[Show her the room you definitely do not keep for your nephew -> WeekOne]]

:: MakeHerAsk {"title":"One Night / The Cost of Asking","scene":"kitchen"}
@do $trust -= 1
@do $leverage += 2
@do $notes.push({id: $notes.length, text: "Mars said please. Lenora kept the word like collateral."})

@Chapter("ONE NIGHT", "The cost of asking")

You leave the chain on until Mars says the word **please**. She says it flatly,
without handing you the satisfaction of sounding small.

You release the chain. She notices that you notice.

“One night,” you say.

“One night,” Mars agrees, which is the first contract neither of you intends to honor.

[[Point her toward the spare room -> WeekOne]]

:: MotelMoney {"title":"One Night / A Clean Boundary","scene":"yard"}
@do $debt += 80
@do $honesty += 1
@do $notes.push({id: $notes.length, text: "Lenora tried to purchase a clean boundary for eighty dollars."})

@Chapter("ONE NIGHT", "A clean boundary")

You offer eighty dollars for a motel. Mars looks at the bills, then at the tow truck.

“You know Cal will take this if I go back for my things.”

“Then don't go back.”

“That easy?”

“No. That simple.”

Mars pockets the money and sleeps in the truck outside your house. At six, you find
her repairing your front gate in yesterday's clothes. At seven, you make coffee for two.

[[Let the boundary become a guest room -> WeekOne]]

:: WeekOne {"title":"Chapter I / The Room That Is Not Waiting","scene":"kitchen"}
@action inspectRoom() {
  @do $sawJulesRoom = true;
  @do $trust += 1;
  @do $notes.push({id: $notes.length, text: "Mars found Jules's jacket inside Lenora's so-called renewal room."});
}

# The room that is not waiting

@Chapter("CHAPTER I · DAY FOUR", "Mutual aid, with conditions")

The spare room contains a folding cot, discount crystals, seven unopened self-help
books, and a sign reading **PATRIARCHAL ENERGY QUARANTINE**. In the closet hangs the
old varsity jacket of your nephew Jules, now a wealthy crisis manager who returns
calls through an assistant.

Mars says the room is waiting for him.

You say it is evidence, not a shrine.

@region roomEvidence {
  @if (!$sawJulesRoom) {
    [[Look behind the quarantine sign => inspectRoom]]
  } @else {
    ::: discovery {.discovery}
    Behind the sign is a pencil mark: **JULES · 16 · STILL GROWING**. Mars does not
    make a joke. Her restraint feels more invasive than a joke would have.
    :::
  }
}

By the fourth morning, Mars has repaired the faucet and moved a plastic basket under
the bathroom sink. You move it from the left side to the right so the house will know
who is in charge.

@Pulse()

[[Invite her to the Wednesday circle -> WednesdayCircle]]

